= Solution
{parent=2}

The integrand is an <even function>, since both $x$ and $\sin x$ are <odd functions>. Using <integration by parts>,
$$
\int_{-\pi/2}^{\pi/2}x\sin x\,dx
=2[-x\cos x+\sin x]_0^{\pi/2}
=\boxed{2}.
$$
The endpoint cosine terms vanish, leaving the sine contribution.

= 3
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

The first <partial derivatives> factor as
$$
f_x=4x(x^2+2y^2-1),\qquad f_y=4y(2x^2+1).
$$
Both vanish at the origin, verifying that it is a <stationary point>. Since $2x^2+1>0$, every <stationary point> has $y=0$; the first equation then gives $x=0,\pm1$. Thus one requested additional point is $\boxed{(1,0)}$; the other is $\boxed{(-1,0)}$.

= 4
{parent=Paper 2}
