= Solution
{parent=a}

In <plane polar coordinates>, $r$ is distance from the origin and $\theta$ is the angle from positive $x$ toward positive $y$. The conversion to <Cartesian coordinates> is
$$
\boxed{x=r\cos\theta,\qquad y=r\sin\theta}.
$$
Conversely $r=\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$, with the angular coordinate undefined at the origin.

= b
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Multiplying the polar equation by its denominator gives $Ar-Bx=1$. Squaring and using $r^2=x^2+y^2$ yields
$$
(A^2-B^2)x^2+A^2y^2-2Bx=1.
$$
Take $C=\sqrt{A^2-B^2}>0$. <Completing the square> gives
$$
C^2\left(x-\frac{B}{C^2}\right)^2+A^2y^2
=1+\frac{B^2}{C^2}=\frac{A^2}{C^2}.
$$
The standard <ellipse> equation is consequently
$$
\boxed{\frac{(x-B/C^2)^2}{A^2/C^4}+\frac{y^2}{1/C^2}=1},
$$
whose centre is $\boxed{(B/C^2,0)}$. Squaring has introduced no unwanted branch: on this <ellipse> the minimum of $1+Bx$ is $A/(A+B)>0$, so $Ar=1+Bx$ follows with the correct positive sign.

= c
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Read the squared semiaxis lengths from the denominators in the standard equation obtained in (b). With the positive choice of $C$ there,
$$
\boxed{a=\frac{A}{C^2},\qquad b=\frac1C}.
$$
Since $C^2=A^2-B^2<A^2$, the horizontal semiaxis $a$ is the larger. Thus these are the <semi-major and semi-minor axes>, respectively.

