= Solution
{parent=a}

Differentiate the sum of <kinetic energy> and <potential energy> using the <dot product> product rule:
$$
\frac{dE}{dt}=m\dot{\mathbf r}\cdot\ddot{\mathbf r}
+k\mathbf r\cdot\dot{\mathbf r}
=\dot{\mathbf r}\cdot(m\ddot{\mathbf r}+k\mathbf r)=0.
$$
The equations of motion make the bracket zero. Hence the <mechanical energy> is **constant in <time>**. The central <restoring force> can exchange kinetic and <potential energy> but supplies no change in their sum.

= b
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Set $\omega=\sqrt{k/m}$. Each coordinate satisfies the <harmonic oscillator equation>, so the <general solution> is
$$
x=A\cos\omega t+B\sin\omega t,\qquad
y=C\cos\omega t+D\sin\omega t.
$$
The four initial data give $A=X_0$, $B=C=0$, and $D=V_0/\omega$. Thus
$$
\boxed{x(t)=X_0\cos\omega t,\qquad y(t)=\frac{V_0}{\omega}\sin\omega t}.
$$
This is the position component of the <isotropic harmonic oscillator flow>. Substitution also gives the constant <energy> $E=(kX_0^2+mV_0^2)/2$.

= c
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The stated condition is equivalent to $(V_0/\omega)^2=X_0^2$. Therefore the solution from (b) obeys
$$
x(t)^2+y(t)^2=X_0^2(\cos^2\omega t+\sin^2\omega t)=X_0^2.
$$
For nonzero initial data this is a **<circle> centred at the origin with radius $|X_0|$**. The sign of $X_0V_0$ chooses its direction of traversal. If $X_0=V_0=0$, the same algebra gives only the stationary origin, a degenerate radius-zero case rather than a genuine circular orbit.

