Let $N=\sqrt8\,A$ be the unscaled <matrix>. Expansion along its third row gives
$$
\det N=2(\sqrt2+\sqrt{18})+2(\sqrt{18}+\sqrt2)=16\sqrt2.
$$
Scaling a three-by-three <matrix> by $1/\sqrt8$ scales its <determinant> by $(\sqrt8)^{-3}$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\det A=\frac{16\sqrt2}{(\sqrt8)^3}=1}.
$$

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The three columns of $N=\sqrt8\,A$ are $(\sqrt3,1,2)$, $(-\sqrt2,\sqrt6,0)$, and $(-\sqrt3,-1,2)$. Each has squared <Euclidean norm> eight. Their pairwise <dot products> are
$$
-\sqrt6+\sqrt6=0,\qquad -3-1+4=0,\qquad \sqrt6-\sqrt6=0.
$$
Hence $N^{\mathsf T}N=8I$, so $\boxed{A^{\mathsf T}A=I}$. Thus $A$ is an <orthogonal matrix>, with $A^{-1}=A^{\mathsf T}$. The <determinant> from (i) places it in the determinant-one component.

= b
{parent=14}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <transpose> rule gives $(BC)^{\mathsf T}=C^{\mathsf T}B^{\mathsf T}$. If $C$ is <orthogonal>, $C^{-1}=C^{\mathsf T}$, and the identity becomes
$$
C^{\mathsf T}(B^{\mathsf T}+B)=0.
$$
Multiplying on the left by $C$ yields $\boxed{B^{\mathsf T}=-B}$. This is precisely the defining condition for an <antisymmetric matrix>.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The inverse appearing in the assumption requires $\det C\ne0$. Taking <determinants> of $(BC)^{\mathsf T}=-C^{-1}B$ gives
$$
\det B\,\det C=-\frac{\det B}{\det C},
$$
where the minus sign comes from the odd <matrix> order. Multiplying by $\det C$ gives $\det B((\det C)^2+1)=0$. Since $C$ is real, the second factor is positive. Thus $\boxed{\det B=0}$ and $B$ is a <singular matrix>. This is the <singularity forced by an odd-dimensional transpose-inverse identity>, with the roles of the two <matrix> letters exchanged.

= 15
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=15}

Use the real <Fourier series> convention $f=a_0/2+\sum_{n\geq1}(a_n\cos nx+b_n\sin nx)$. The <function> is an <even function>, so $b_n=0$. For noninteger real $\mu$,
$$
a_0=\frac2\pi\int_0^\pi\cos(\mu x)\,dx=\frac{2\sin\pi\mu}{\pi\mu}.
$$
For $n\geq1$, the product-to-sum identity gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
a_n&=\frac1\pi\left(\frac{\sin\pi(\mu-n)}{\mu-n}
+\frac{\sin\pi(\mu+n)}{\mu+n}\right)\\
&=\frac{2\mu(-1)^n\sin\pi\mu}{\pi(\mu^2-n^2)}.
\end{aligned}
$$
Thus the <Fourier series of a noninteger-frequency cosine> is
$$
\boxed{f(x)=\frac{\sin\pi\mu}{\pi\mu}
+\frac{2\mu\sin\pi\mu}{\pi}\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}
\frac{(-1)^n\cos nx}{\mu^2-n^2}}.
$$
Its <Fourier coefficients> are $O(n^{-2})$, giving <absolute convergence> and <uniform convergence>. The periodic <function> is continuous at its joins because the two endpoint cosine values coincide, so the <Dirichlet-Jordan convergence theorem> gives this value at every point.

Putting $\mu=1/3$ gives
$$
\boxed{f(x)=\frac{3\sqrt3}{2\pi}-\frac{3\sqrt3}{\pi}
\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{(-1)^n\cos nx}{9n^2-1}}.
$$
Evaluate at $x=0$, where $f(0)=1$, and multiply by $\pi/\sqrt3$:
$$
\frac\pi{\sqrt3}=\frac32-3\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{(-1)^n}{9n^2-1}
=\frac32-\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{(-1)^n}{3n^2-1/3}.
$$
Comparing with the required form gives $\boxed{A=3,\qquad B=3}$.

= 16
{parent=Paper 2}
