= Solution
{parent=16}

The first field is $\mathbf F_1=(z-y,x,-x)$, so direct <curl> calculation gives
$$
\boxed{\nabla\times\mathbf F_1=(0,2,2)=2\mathbf a}.
$$
It is **not conservative** because its <curl> is nonzero. The second is a radial <gradient> field:
$$
\mathbf F_2=\nabla\left[-\frac1{\sqrt{1+r^2}}\right],
\qquad\boxed{\nabla\times\mathbf F_2=0}.
$$
The <scalar> expression is a <smooth function> and single-valued on all of space, so $\mathbf F_2$ is a <conservative vector field>. Its <potential of a conservative vector field> is $\Phi=-(1+r^2)^{-1/2}$. Parts (a) and (b) check path dependence directly.

= a
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Parametrize the segment by $\mathbf r(t)=(1-t,t,0)$, $0\leq t\leq1$, so $\mathbf r'=(-1,1,0)$. For the first field, $\mathbf F_1=(-t,1-t,-1+t)$ and its <dot product> with the tangent is one. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\int_{\rm segment}\mathbf F_1\cdot d\mathbf r=1}.
$$
For the second field, use the <fundamental theorem for line integrals>. The endpoints both have $r=1$, so their potential values agree and
$$
\boxed{\int_{\rm segment}\mathbf F_2\cdot d\mathbf r=0}.
$$
Directly, its parameter integrand is $(2t-1)/[1+(1-t)^2+t^2]^{3/2}$, which changes sign under $t\mapsto1-t$ and integrates to zero.

= b
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The shorter circular arc lies in the $x$-$y$ <plane> and can be written $\mathbf r(\theta)=(\cos\theta,\sin\theta,0)$, $0\leq\theta\leq\pi/2$. The first field is $(-\sin\theta,\cos\theta,-\cos\theta)$, whose <dot product> with $\mathbf r'$ is one. Hence
$$
\boxed{\int_{\rm arc}\mathbf F_1\cdot d\mathbf r=\frac\pi2}.
$$
The second field is radial while the circular tangent is perpendicular to the radius, so $\boxed{\int_{\rm arc}\mathbf F_2\cdot d\mathbf r=0}$. Thus the first field gives different values, $1$ and $\pi/2$, along the two paths, whereas the conservative second field gives the same endpoint difference.

As a check using <Stokes theorem>, the arc followed by the reversed segment encloses <area> $\pi/4-1/2$ in the <plane>. The constant <curl> has upward component two, giving circulation $2(\pi/4-1/2)=\pi/2-1$, exactly the difference above.

\Image[paper-2-line-integral-paths.png]
{title=Straight and circular paths with the same endpoints, enclosing the area responsible for their circulation difference}

