Split the integrand into its three coordinate contributions. Each integrates independently over the rectangular box:
$$
\begin{aligned}
I&=\left(\frac23\right)(4)(6)+(2)\left(\frac{16}{3}\right)(6)
+(2)(4)(18)\\
&=16+64+144=\boxed{224}.
\end{aligned}
$$
For example, the last factor is $\int_{-3}^3z^2dz=18$. No spherical or cylindrical Jacobian is needed for this Cartesian <integral>.

= b
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The region is the <triangle> $0\leq y\leq x\leq1$. Reversing the integration order makes the inner <integral> elementary:
$$
\int_0^1\int_y^1e^{x^2}\,dx\,dy
=\int_0^1\int_0^xe^{x^2}\,dy\,dx
=\int_0^1xe^{x^2}\,dx
=\boxed{\frac{e-1}{2}}.
$$
The integrand is continuous on this compact <triangle>, so <Fubini theorem> justifies exchanging the order.

= c
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

In <cylindrical coordinates>, the two surfaces meet when $9-r^2=r^2$, giving $r=3/\sqrt2$ and $z=9/2$. The solid has $0\leq r\leq3/\sqrt2$, $0\leq\theta\leq2\pi$, and $r^2\leq z\leq9-r^2$. With <volume element> $r\,dz\,dr\,d\theta$,
$$
\boxed{V=2\pi\int_0^{3/\sqrt2}(9-2r^2)r\,dr
=2\pi\left[\frac92r^2-\frac12r^4\right]_0^{3/\sqrt2}
=\frac{81\pi}{4}}.
$$
The upper-minus-lower height is nonnegative throughout that radial interval.

= 19
{parent=Paper 2}
