= a
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Fix the total <area> at $A_0>0$. Apply <Lagrange multipliers> to $V=\pi a^2\ell$ with constraint $G=2\pi a(a+\ell)=A_0$. The two <derivative> equations are
$$
2\pi a\ell=\lambda(4\pi a+2\pi\ell),\qquad
\pi a^2=\lambda\,2\pi a.
$$
Since $a>0$, the second gives $\lambda=a/2$. Substitution in the first gives $\ell=2a$. The constraint then yields $a^2=A_0/(6\pi)$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{V_{\max}=2\pi\left(\frac{A_0}{6\pi}\right)^{3/2}
=\frac13\sqrt{\frac{A_0^3}{6\pi}},\qquad C=6}.
$$
For completeness, eliminating $\ell$ makes $V=(A_0/2)a-\pi a^3$ on $0<a<\sqrt{A_0/(2\pi)}$. Its <derivative> changes from positive to negative at the calculated radius and its <volume> tends to zero at both domain endpoints. Thus this stationary <cylinder> is indeed the maximum. It is the fixed-area case of <optimal aspect ratios of closed cylinders>.

= b
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Divide the objective <area> by $2\pi$, which does not change its maximizer, and use $F=a^2+a\ell$ with constraint $G=a^2+\ell^2/4=1$. The <Lagrange multiplier> equations are
$$
2a+\ell=2\lambda a,\qquad a=\frac{\lambda\ell}{2}.
$$
At a nondegenerate <cylinder> $a,\ell>0$, the second gives $\lambda=2a/\ell$. Eliminating it gives $\ell^2+2a\ell=4a^2$. Hence $q=\ell/a$ satisfies $q^2+2q-4=0$. Select its positive root:
$$
\boxed{\frac\ell a=\sqrt5-1,\qquad D=-1,\qquad E=5}.
$$
This is the sphere-inscribed case of <optimal aspect ratios of closed cylinders>. One may check maximality from $A(q)=2\pi(1+q)/(1+q^2/4)$: its <derivative> has the sign of $4-2q-q^2$, so it changes from positive to negative at the selected root.

